Key structural and metabolic changes
Metabolic shifts in the heart
Under chronic hypoxia the heart switches fuel sources. Because fatty acid oxidation uses more oxygen than glucose oxidation, the heart begins to prefer glucose as its fuel. Mitochondria change: smaller size, altered number, and shifts in enzyme expression occur. These adaptations help the heart squeeze more out of limited oxygen. But when other stressors (like high blood pressure, diabetes or lung disease) join in, these adaptations may become maladaptive.
Muscle and vessel-level remodeling
The heart muscle (myocardium) may thicken (hypertrophy) or the walls may stiffen as a response to continuous low oxygen and higher workload. Vessels feeding the heart and in the lungs respond too: they may narrow or remodel under hypoxic stress, raising resistance and making the heart work harder. Over time this can reduce the heart’s efficiency and contribute to symptoms like fatigue, breathlessness or fluid retention.
